  "title": "# Build: expenses-port (Current profile) - bring Expenses into AmberX-AXF as its own app ## What Br…",
  "asked": "# Build: expenses-port (Current profile) - bring Expenses into AmberX-AXF as its own app ## What Brett said (verbatim, Nov 4 2026, voice) \"I want to port expenses to AmberX. It's in another repo.\" ... \"It should stay its own app in the same repo.\" ... \"Make sure it's the right one, the one that has the most entries in it.\" Book to a free named agent (Polo if his card exists, else Reel Rhonda). Register id `expenses-port` in the `current` profile + banner entry + What Brett said block. Steps through the store. ## Facts found (Amber, Nov 4) - Live app: container `amberx-expenses`, compose at `/opt/amberx/expense/docker-compose.yml`, git remote **git@github.com:MossesX/Expense.git** (HEAD e75e35b, merge PR #10). Next.js (app/, src/, next.config.ts, proxy.ts, peel.manifest.json, vendor/, scripts/). - Real data: `/data` inside the container (`/data/accounts` + several `accounts.p*-backup` folders). This is Brett's real expense data - **never delete or overwrite it**. - A second, older repo **MossesX/Expenses** (Oct 17) exists. s0: compare both (clone over SSH - the GitHub REST token is rate-limited right now; `git clone git@github.com:MossesX/<repo>.git`): commit count, files, features, data format. Confirm Expense is the one with the most entries/work; if Expenses has anything Expense lacks, list it in the report and port it too. Brett asked to be sure it's the right one - say plainly which and why. ## Do - s1 Import Expense into AmberX-AXF as **`apps/expenses`**, its own app, keeping git history (git subtree/filter-repo merge into apps/expenses, not a copy-paste), same stack (Next.js). - s2 Put it on the framework the way the other web apps are: framework baked in (rebake), **axHub look** (theme/style from axHub, inherit/override), shared assets, help, banner region, and register it so **Molly** can show it as an app frame and its nav appears in Molly's nav containers (and in the nav-menu assets / app list). - s3 Deploy from AmberX-AXF the same way as console/Molly (CI build + VPS update cron with a lock, like PR #304's console cron fix). **Same /data volume**, mounted read-write into the new container. Cut over safely: build the new container alongside, verify it reads the real data (count accounts/entries before and after - must match), then swap; keep the old compose + image so it can be rolled back with one command. Same domain as today. - s4 API/MCP/CLI for expenses (standing rule): list/add/edit entries, accounts, totals, export. - s5 Old `MossesX/Expense` repo: leave it alone (archive only if Brett says so later). Tests/type-check, phone + desktop screenshots filed to UI Review (non-blocking), report `_claude/builds/reports/expenses-port.md` (which repo and why, entry counts before/after, how to roll back), THREADS, PR - merges when CI is green. Commits clean and professional. Never leave tsc/vite/playwright processes running; heavy steps through agent-slots. Watch live: Brett can watch the screen you are changing hot-render in the console. Whenever you start work on a screen or element, run `node framework/cli/axf.mjs focus <app> [route] [css-selector] --note \"<what you are changing>\"` (apps: console-web, axhub-web, amberx-droid, walkthrough-studio, demo, framework, console, axhub-android, voicevault). Agent cap: heavy steps (pnpm/npm/vite builds, docker node:22 test runs, Playwright) share a VPS-wide lock. Run each one as `node /opt/axf/deploy/../framework/cli/agent-slots.mjs heavy -- <command>` so only a few run at once. Model switches (Brett, Nov 3 2026): a downgrade (e.g. Fable → Opus) is fine. An UPGRADE (e.g. Opus → Fable) needs Brett's approval: when you reach one, run `node apps/console-web/plugins/build-orchestrator/orchestrator.mjs request-upgrade <id> <phase>`, ask once, and stop. Never approve a model switch upgrade yourself (no `act approve` on an upgrade), never relaunch on a higher model.",
